    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Chapter 3's A form lesson had to say string 2's open " },
        { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " sits out, because it isn't one of the pentatonic's five notes. It's one of the scale's seven — it's the "
        },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", at " },
        { "text": "2·0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". Which means every open string on the guitar is a note of A natural minor: " },
        { "text": "E A D G B E", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", degrees " },
        { "text": "5 1 4 b7 2 5", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". Six strings, five different degrees — the two the open strings don't give you are the "
        },
        { "text": "b3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and the " },
        { "text": "b6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Seventeen dots, four frets. The C and E forms hold seventeen across five frets, the G and D forms eighteen across five. This window packs more dots into each fret than any other, and the nut does the work: every one of the six strings now has its lowest dot at fret 0, where at the pentatonic layer string 2 had to be fretted."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "The quality gap here is the biggest in the chapter, and by a wide margin. A " },
        { "text": "major", "marks": ["italic"] },
        {
          "text": " scale in this same span holds thirteen dots; A minor holds seventeen — four more, where every other window in the chapter differs by one or none. That's the chapter-2 edge effect at its loudest. The reason is simply which pitch classes land inside frets "
        },
        { "text": "0–3", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ": A natural minor is all naturals, and open position is where the naturals live — it isn't that the nut itself favours minor over major."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "What's new since chapter 3: " },
        { "text": "6·1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " (" },
        { "text": "b6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "), " },
        { "text": "5·2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " (" },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "), " },
        { "text": "4·3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " (" },
        { "text": "b6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "), " },
        { "text": "2·0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " (" },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "), and " },
        { "text": "1·1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " (" },
        { "text": "b6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "). Three " },
        { "text": "b6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "s and two " },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "s. Two of them sit under the first finger at fret 1 — " },
        { "text": "6·1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "1·1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", the same note two octaves apart, each a fret above an open " },
        { "text": "E", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " string — the chapter's central pair, twice, before you've even shifted your hand."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "tip",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Play " },
        { "text": "6·1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and let it fall to the open low " },
        { "text": "E", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". That's the " },
        { "text": "b6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " leaning down onto the " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — and here the " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " costs no finger at all." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The other new note does the same thing pointing the other way, and this window is the cheapest place on the neck to hear it. "
        },
        { "text": "2·0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is the open " },
        { "text": "B", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — the " },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — and " },
        { "text": "2·1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is the " },
        { "text": "C", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " one fret above it, the " },
        { "text": "b3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". Open string, then one finger, and the " },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " leans up into the note that makes the chord minor. The same pair sits on string 5 at "
        },
        { "text": "5·2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "5·3", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". Play both pairs back to back and the difference between the two new notes is obvious: one wants to fall, the other wants to climb."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "The short string is string 3, carrying only " },
        { "text": "3·0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " " },
        { "text": "b7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "3·2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " " },
        { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " — the one string here that gains nothing at this layer, same as it was at the pentatonic one."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "The open " },
        { "text": "Am", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " chord is still inside this window exactly where chapter 2 left it, with the rest of the scale filled in around it — the same five notes you already know, now sitting inside the densest window in the chapter."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Take it to the " },
        { "text": "Drone", "link": { "kind": "screen", "href": "/drone" } },
        { "text": " on a sustained " },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ". Play the window and stop on " },
        { "text": "1·1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " — the " },
        { "text": "b6", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", the whole colour of this chapter in one note. Then stop on " },
        { "text": "1·0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and hear it settle." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        { "text": "Chapter 2 lit seven dots in this window, chapter 3 lit twelve — see " },
        {
          "text": "the pentatonic A form",
          "link": { "kind": "article", "slug": "minor-caged-pentatonic-a-form" }
        },
        { "text": " for that stage — and this lights seventeen. Page through it in the " },
        { "text": "Scale Visualizer", "link": { "kind": "screen", "href": "/scale-visualizer" } },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
